=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
The root cause is a set of injected iptables rules in the FORWARD chain of router6 that block virtually all transit traffic arriving on eth2 (the 10.50.1.0/24 inter-router link to router3, which is the only WAN-side path into router6 from the rest of the topology). Specifically, three DROP rules were added — one for TCP, one for UDP, and one matching all remaining protocols (proto 0, catching ICMP) — all scoped to `-i eth2`. The only exemption is one ACCEPT rule permitting TCP port 80 from Location A (172.16.10.0/24) to a single host (172.18.51.1).

The effect is complete bilateral isolation of Location B: inbound traffic from Location C and Location A toward PC15–PC18 and the laptops is dropped the moment it enters router6.eth2. Conversely, even if Location B hosts try to initiate sessions outward, the reply packets re-enter via eth2 and are dropped by the same rules — confirmed by PC15 also failing to ping PC4. The 4 blocked ICMP packets (4 × 84 bytes = 336 bytes) logged in rule 4 exactly match our PC11→PC15 ping probe.

Routing, interfaces, and L2 infrastructure (switch4/5/6, mswitch2) are all healthy. Location C can still reach Location A (paths not routed through router6). The rules exist only in kernel memory — there is no `/etc/iptables/rules.v4` on disk — confirming a runtime injection rather than a configuration-file change.

Corrective fix: on router6, delete rules 2–4 from the FORWARD chain (`iptables -D FORWARD 4`, then 3, then 2, working backwards to preserve numbering), and restore the intended baseline B→A block if required by policy.
=== END ===